# IOSTAT(1M)

## NAME

iostat - report I/O statistics

## SYNOPSIS

| **iostat** [ **-t** ] [ **-i** ] [ interval [ count ] ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Iostat* delves into the system and reports certain statistics kept about input-output
activity. For typewriters collectively, the number of input and output characters are counted.
Also, on each clock tick, a tally is made in one of 32 slots, chosen by which devices had an
exchange outstanding crossed with whether the system was executing in user mode, in `nice`
(background) user mode, in system mode, or idle. Each of the four categories reported below is
therefore the sum of eight of those slots, and each device's busy share is the sum of the slots
with its own bit set.

The optional *interval* argument causes *iostat* to report once each *interval* seconds. The
first report is for all time since a reboot and each subsequent report is for the last interval
only.

The optional *count* argument restricts the number of reports. With an *interval* and no
*count*, *iostat* reports until it is stopped. With neither, it prints one report and exits.

With no option argument *iostat* gives, for each instrumented device, the percentage of time it
had an exchange outstanding (**%bsy**), the exchanges it completed per second (**tps**) and the
words those exchanges moved per second (**wps**); and then the percentage of time the system
has spent in each of the four categories mentioned above.

There are two devices. **MD** is the magnetic disk of **kernel/dev/md.c**, **all of its units
together,** and **MB** is the drum, which is also the paging store.

The following options are available:

**-t**
: Report the number of characters of terminal IO per second as well.

**-i**
: Report the percentage of time spent in each of the four categories mentioned above, one per
  line and named.

## FILES

None. *Iostat* reads **dk_time**, **dk_numb**, **dk_wds**, **tk_nin** and **tk_nout** through
kctl(2) and opens no device.

## SEE ALSO

kctl(2), vmstat(1), pstat(1M), ps(1)

## BUGS

**Note: a device is a controller, not a drive.** v7's *iostat* named three disks and reported
milliseconds per seek and per transfer for each. **dk_busy** is three bits wide here because it
is the low three bits of the subscript **dk_time** is tallied by, and **kernel/dev/md.c**
addresses 64 drives; so **MD** is the disks together and **MB** the drums together, and a
per-drive breakdown would need a wider histogram than **dk_time** is. Seek and transfer times
are not reported at all: the controller returns no timing, and **wps** says what those columns
were for. There is no **cp_time** on this system and none is wanted - **dk_time** is it.

**Note:** v7's **-s** (the 32 raw slots) and **-b** (buffer-cache statistics, out of an
**io_info** structure this kernel does not have) are gone, as are the undocumented **-d** and
**-a**.

**Note:** the clock runs at **HZ** ticks per second, which is 250 here and was 60 on the
PDP-11. Nothing in the report is expressed in ticks, so this shows up only in the resolution of
the percentages.

Things can change while *iostat* is running.
